<div class="chapter-content">
 
<h1 class="title">CAUSAL MEDIATION ANALYSIS FOR NON-CAUSALLY RELATED MEDIATORS</h1>
<h4 class="author">Komi Ayi</h4>
 
<p>In the context of mediation analysis, a researcher may, in full awareness, choose to focus on a single mediator of particular interest among several mediators present between an exposure and an outcome. This approach, which amounts to conducting a simple mediation analysis within a multiple mediation framework, aims to estimate the indirect effect of the exposure through the targeted mediator, as well as the complementary direct effect, representing all other pathways of influence, including those passing through the remaining mediators.</p>
 
<p>As emphasized in the introduction, in the context of Figure \(\ref{figmed:fig2}\), where the mediator of interest shares an unmeasured common cause with other mediators, the classical mediation analysis yields biased estimators of the direct and indirect effects of the targeted mediator. Indeed, this common cause acts as an unmeasured confounder between the mediator of interest and the outcome, thereby violating the Sequential Ignorability Assumption (SIA) hypothesis \(\eqref{hypo.1}\). The following Chapter 2 introduces a methodology designed to circumvent this issue.</p>
 
<p>This chapter proposes a methodological framework to perform a simple mediation analysis for a particular mediator, while accounting for the presence of a second mediator presumed to be non-causally related to the first. Within this context, we therefore assume that both mediators are observed.</p>
 
<h1 id="contextual-framework">Contextual Framework</h1>
 
<p>Let us consider the vector \(\boldsymbol{X}\), which represents the pre-exposure variables, \(T\) a binary exposure variable, where \(T = 1\) indicates exposure and \(T = 0\) denotes the absence of exposure, along with the mediators \(M_1, M_2\) involved in the causal mechanism. The variable \(U\) represents an unmeasured common cause of the mediators, and \(Y\) the outcome. The causal structure linking these elements is depicted in the causal diagram of Figure 1.</p>
 
<div style="text-align: center;">
  <p><img src="figure_tikz.PNG" alt="Causal DAG with two mediators sharing a common cause" style="max-width: 100%;" /></p>
  <p><em>Figure 1: Causal DAG of mediation analysis with two mediators sharing a common cause.</em></p>
</div>
 
<p>Let \(M_1\) denote the mediator of interest. We denote by \(\boldsymbol{M}\) the vector of the two observed mediators. In the causal diagram of Figure 1, \(M_1\) is influenced by \(\boldsymbol{X}\), \(T\), and the unmeasured common cause \(U\). The latter simultaneously affects both \(M_1\) and the mediator \(M_2\), thereby inducing a dependence between \(M_1\) and \(M_2\). Focusing on a particular mediator leads to a restricted analysis, in which we concentrate on the mediator \(M_1\), as illustrated in the diagram of Figure 2, representing the direct and indirect causal relationships between \(T\), \(M_1\), and \(Y\). The pathways \(T \to Y\) and \(T \to M_2 \to Y\) constitute the pathways contributing to the direct effect of \(T\) on \(Y\) when considering \(M_1\) as the targeted mediator, whereas \(T \to M_1 \to Y\) corresponds to the indirect pathway via \(M_1\). In addition to the direct link from \(M_1\) to \(Y\), a non-causal pathway exists between \(M_1\) and \(Y\) that passes through \(U\), namely \(M_1 \leftarrow U \to M_2 \to Y\). This pathway represents a confounding pathway between \(M_1\) and \(Y\) arising from the presence of the unmeasured common cause \(U\), thereby posing a problem for the use of the standard simple mediation approach.</p>
 
<div style="text-align: center;">
  <p><img src="figure2_tikz.PNG" alt="Simple analysis DAG for M1" style="max-width: 100%;" /></p>
  <p><em>Figure 2: DAG of the simple analysis of \(M_1\).</em></p>
</div>
 
<p>Our objective is therefore to identify and estimate the simple natural direct and indirect effects of \(M_1\), while overcoming the difficulty arising from the presence of the unmeasured variable \(U\).</p>
 
<p>In the literature, a classical solution for mitigating confounding bias in an analysis is to adjust or stratify on the confounders. However, since \(U\) is unmeasured, this strategy cannot be adopted. Adjusting on \(M_2\) could effectively eliminate the confounding between the mediator of interest and the outcome arising from the opening of the pathway \(M_1 \leftarrow U \to M_2 \to Y\). Nevertheless, this strategy gives rise to another issue: it blocks the causal pathway \(T \to M_2 \to Y\), thereby introducing bias in the estimation of the direct effect of \(T\) on \(Y\) with \(M_1\) as the mediator of interest. This creates a dilemma, as one must simultaneously adjust and not adjust for \(M_2\).</p>
 
<p>To resolve this dilemma, our approach initially consists of incorporating the mediator \(M_2\) into the analysis by adapting the composition assumption of <span class="citation">Halpern and Pearl (2005)</span> in order to account for this complex causal structure.</p>
 
<h2 id="incorporation-m2">Incorporation of the Mediator \(M_2\) into the Simple Mediation Analysis</h2>
 
<h3 id="definition-effects">Definition of the Direct and Indirect Effects in the Simple Mediation Analysis</h3>
 
<p>In this section, we incorporate the mediator \(M_2\) into the simple mediation analysis in order to assess the direct and indirect effects of \(M_1\) while accounting for the dependence between the two mediators. Let \(\boldsymbol{M}(t)\) denote the vector of potential values of the mediators when the exposure is set to \(t\). Similarly, \(Y(t,\boldsymbol{M}(t'))\) denotes the potential value of the outcome when the exposure is set to \(t\) and the mediators take their potential values under exposure \(t'\). Finally, \(Y(t,M_1(t'),M_2(t''))\) represents the potential value of the outcome when \(M_1\) and \(M_2\) are respectively set to their potential values under exposures \(t'\) and \(t''\).</p>
 
<p>The composition assumption is expressed as follows:</p>
 
<p>$$\textbf{(H}_0\textbf{)} \quad Y(t,M_1(t'))=Y(t,M_1(t'),\boldsymbol{M}_2(t)),\;\forall\,t,t'.$$</p>
 
<p>This assumption states that the potential value of \(Y\) when \(T\) is set to \(t\) and \(M_1\) takes its potential value under another exposure \(t'\) is equal to the potential value of \(Y\) when \(T\) is set to \(t\), \(M_1\) takes its value under \(t'\), and the vector \(M_2\) takes its potential value under \(t\). In other words, the intervention on \(M_2\) has no additional effect on the potential outcome.</p>
 
<p>Under this assumption, the direct and indirect effects of \(M_1\) are defined as follows:</p>
 
<p>$$\zeta = \mathbb{E}\left\{Y(1,M_1(0),M_2(1))-Y(0,M_1(0),M_2(0))\right\},$$</p>
 
<p>$$\delta = \mathbb{E}\left\{Y(1,M_1(1),M_2(1))-Y(1,M_1(0),M_2(1))\right\}.$$</p>
 
<p>The indirect effect \(\delta\) measures the impact of \(T\) on \(Y\) through the specific variations of \(M_1\), while keeping the mediator \(M_2\) fixed at its potential value under exposure \(T=1\). It is equal to the individual indirect effect of this mediator within the framework of a multiple mediation analysis. In contrast, the direct effect \(\zeta\) with \(M_1\) as the targeted mediator captures the impact of \(T\) on \(Y\), while controlling solely \(M_1\) at a fixed value. It should be noted that the change in the state of the exposure \(T\) also affects \(M_2\), which means that the direct effect associated with the mediator \(M_1\) also incorporates the effect of the mediator \(M_2\) on the outcome.</p>
 
<p>Consequently, the direct effect \(\zeta\) can be decomposed into two distinct components. First, the impact of the exposure on \(Y\), while keeping \(M_2\) fixed at its potential value under \(T=0\):</p>
 
<p>$$\mathbb{E}\{Y(1,M_1(0),M_2(0))-Y(0,M_1(0),M_2(0))\}.$$</p>
 
<p>This expression corresponds to the joint direct effect within the framework of a multiple mediation analysis involving both mediators. Second, the impact of the exposure on \(Y\) through the sole change in the state of \(M_2\):</p>
 
<p>$$\mathbb{E}\{Y(1,M_1(0),M_2(1))-Y(1,M_1(0),M_2(0))\},$$</p>
 
<p>which corresponds to the indirect effect through \(M_2\) alone <span class="citation">(Jérolon et al. 2021)</span>.</p>
 
<p>Thus, under the composition assumption, the natural effects \(\delta\) and \(\zeta\) are determined as functions of the joint direct effect and the individual indirect effects derived from a multiple mediation analysis. Within these effects, the potential outcomes \(Y(1,M_1(0),M_2(1))\) and \(Y(1,M_1(0),M_2(0))\), required to compute \(\delta\) and \(\zeta\), are not observable in the data. Consequently, these natural effects are not identifiable without introducing additional assumptions.</p>
 
<h3 id="identification-effects">Identification of the Natural Effects \(\delta\) and \(\zeta\)</h3>
 
<p>The simple natural effects \(\delta\) and \(\zeta\), defined as functions of the joint direct effect and the individual indirect effects in a multiple mediation analysis, rely for their identification on the SIMMA assumption <span class="citation">(Jérolon et al. 2021)</span>.</p>
 
<p>Under this assumption, the direct and indirect effects can be partially identified non-parametrically through the following formulas:</p>
 
<p>$$\begin{aligned}
\zeta = &\int_{\mathcal{X}}\Bigg(\int_{\mathcal{M}} \mathbb{E}[Y|T=1,M_1=m_1,M_2=m_2,\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}]\,dF_{(M_1(0),M_2(1))|\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}}(m_1,m_2) \\
& -\int_{\mathcal{M}} \mathbb{E}[Y|T=0,M_1=m_1,M_2=m_2,\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}]\,dF_{(M_1,M_2)|T=0,\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}}(m_1,m_2)\Bigg)dF_{\boldsymbol{X}}(\boldsymbol{x}),
\end{aligned}$$</p>
 
<p>$$\begin{aligned}
\delta = &\int_{\mathcal{X}}\Bigg(\int_{\mathcal{M}} \mathbb{E}[Y|T=1,M_1=m_1,M_2=m_2,\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}]\,dF_{(M_1,M_2)|T=1,\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}}(m_1,m_2) \\
& -\int_{\mathcal{M}} \mathbb{E}[Y|T=1,M_1=m_1,M_2=m_2,\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}]\,dF_{(M_1(0),M_2(1))|\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}}(m_1,m_2)\Bigg)dF_{\boldsymbol{X}}(\boldsymbol{x}),
\end{aligned}$$</p>
 
<p>where \(dF_{\boldsymbol{X}}\) denotes the probability measure of the distribution of \(\boldsymbol{X}\), and \(dF_{\boldsymbol{M}|T=t',\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}}\) the conditional probability measure of \(\boldsymbol{M}\) given that \(T=t'\) and \(\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}\).</p>
 
<p>The complete identification of these simple effects, however, requires additional assumptions regarding the joint distribution of \((M_1(0), M_2(1))|\boldsymbol{X}=\boldsymbol{x}\). In the following section, we present a regression-based approach for continuous variables, along with the identification of the estimators of the effects \(\zeta\) and \(\delta\).</p>
 
<h1 id="references" class="unnumbered">References</h1>
 
<div id="refs" class="references">
  <p>Halpern, Joseph Y., and Judea Pearl. 2005. "Causes and Explanations: A Structural-Model Approach. Part I: Causes." <em>British Journal for the Philosophy of Science</em> 56 (4): 843–87. <a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/bjps/axi147">https://doi.org/10.1093/bjps/axi147</a>.</p>
 
  <p>Jérolon, Allan, Laura Baglietto, Etienne Birmelé, Flora Alarcon, and Vittorio Perduca. 2021. "Causal Mediation Analysis in Presence of Multiple Mediators Uncausally Related." <em>The International Journal of Biostatistics</em> 17 (2): 191–221.</p>
</div>
 
